><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Our PField test revealed that novices had a larger peripheral&#xD;field of view; however, when a sports-specific action response&#xD;was required, the expert advantage became apparent. Experts were&#xD;able to respond to a teammate appearing in their periphery at greater&#xD;distance from central vision, and more accurately, suggesting their&#xD;years of practice results in superior action organization and attunement&#xD;to sport specific information appearing in their far periphery and&#xD;better able to use it to support the control of action.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="5" rect="612.000000,371.859650,796.000000,463.859650"
/></highlight
